builtin tool evaluations
========================

one evaluation per builtin tool, parallel to plans/ (same names:
evals/repl_step evaluates plans/repl_step's tool). the two tracks
answer different questions:

- plans/ verify the MECHANISM: the tool does what its description
  says, at every layer (ml unit / scala unit / bridge / e2e).
- evals/ verify the ABSTRACTION: given only a TASK, does an agent
  discover the tool, call it correctly, and recover from its errors
  using nothing but what the server advertises (descriptions,
  schemas, annotations, error texts)?

the evaluations drive the reflection part of the project. a tool can
be green at all four test layers and still be a bad tool — never
picked, picked with wrong arguments, or abandoned after the first
error. when an evaluation shows that, we update the spec and try a
second implementation; the evaluation is the regression test for the
redesign.

principles
----------

- prompts state the task, never the tool. the prompt describes what a
  user wants ("prove this lemma", "why does my theory fail to
  build"), not which tool to call. tool discovery IS the measurement;
  a prompt that names the tool measures nothing.
- one focal tool per evaluation. the agent will legitimately use
  other tools on the way (repl_step's eval needs repl_init); those
  uses are context, only the focal tool is graded. which OTHER tools
  the agent reaches for instead of the focal one is itself signal.
- the agent sees only the advertised surface. no skill files, no
  extra system prompt about the server, in the first round — the
  descriptions must carry the tool. (a second round MAY add the
  planned skills, isabelle-mcp-exploring / isabelle-mcp-verifying,
  to measure how much the skills buy; keep the rounds apart.)
- setup is scripted, not prompted. state the tool needs to exist
  (pre-made repls, a broken theory file on disk) is created by the
  harness before the agent starts, so the prompt stays a pure task
  statement.

grading (per run, read from the transcript)
-------------------------------------------

  outcome     achieved / achieved with detours / failed
  selection   did the agent call the focal tool at all; at the first
              moment it was the right call, or only after exhausting
              alternatives?
  first call  were the arguments right on the first call (names,
              types, the exclusivity rules the schema cannot express)?
  recovery    after a tool error, did the error text alone get the
              agent to a correct retry, or did it loop / give up /
              work around?

a tool passes when outcome is achieved AND selection was unforced AND
recovery never needed more than one retry, across the run set (aim
for >= 3 runs per evaluation; agent behavior is stochastic).

reflection ladder (what to change when an evaluation fails)
-----------------------------------------------------------

failures point at a layer; fix the cheapest layer that explains the
transcript, rerun, escalate only if it recurs:

  1. description — agent never selected the tool, or misread its
     contract: rewrite the description (this is the common case; the
     plans' descriptions are first drafts).
  2. schema/errors — agent selected it but fumbled arguments or
     could not act on an error: tighten inputSchema, name the key in
     the error, add the missing example to the description.
  3. abstraction — agent used it "correctly" and still fought it
     (wrong granularity, surprising state model): spec change,
     second implementation. record the decision in the spec and
     CHANGELOG, keep the evaluation as the acceptance test.
  4. existence — task achieved more naturally without the tool every
     time: candidate for removal or merge; take it to the spec.

harness
-------

run the agent with claude code against the built server:

  claude --mcp-config '{"mcpServers": {"isabelle": {"command":
    "Isabelle2025-2_linux/Isabelle2025-2/bin/isabelle",
    "args": ["mcp_server"]}}}' -p "<prompt>"

first round is manual: run, read the transcript, grade the four axes,
note quotes for the reflection. automation (scripted setup + grader)
only after the template has survived a few tools — evals/repl_list is
the pilot, runnable today since repl_list is implemented.

each evaluation file: focal tool, task, setup, prompt (verbatim),
success (observable end state), signals (what to watch beyond the
four axes, tied to the plan's pinned decisions), reflection (the
specific spec questions this evaluation can settle).

status key: [ ] planned only, [x] run at least once and graded.

